1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript contains 4 regions capable of alternative base-pairing. Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome translates the leader peptide without stalling, thereby covering region 2; this leaves regions 3 and 4 free to form the terminator hairpin that aborts transcription.  

A mutation that prevents region 1 from pairing with region 2 frees region 2 even when the ribosome is covering region 1. Consequently region 2 can pair with region 3, sequestering region 3 and blocking formation of the 3-4 terminator. Transcription therefore continues.  

The remaining choices either favor terminator formation (B), alter termination efficiency without preventing 3-4 pairing (C), or reduce transcription initiation (E); none of them selectively block the 3-4 stem-loop under high-Trp conditions.}

3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader transcript contains four regions capable of alternative base-pairing. The 3:4 stem-loop (followed by the U-run) is the functional attenuator/terminator. Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome translates trpL to its stop codon and thereby sequesters region 2, freeing region 3 to pair with region 4 and form the terminator.

A mutation that abolishes 1:2 pairing leaves region 2 free to pair with the newly transcribed region 3 as soon as region 3 emerges. Consequently the 2:3 antiterminator forms even when the ribosome is not stalled, preventing 3:4 formation and allowing read-through despite high tryptophan. All other options either favor 3:4 formation (B), alter termination efficiency without preventing hairpin formation (C), or are irrelevant to hairpin choice (D, E).}

